## What is Big O?
Big O describes how an algorithm's performance scales as input grows. It answers: "If I double the input, how much slower does it get?"

### Common complexities (fastest to slowest):
- `O(1)` - Constant. Array access by index. Doesn't matter if there's 10 or 10 million items.
- `O(log n)` - Logarithmic. Binary search. Doubles input = one extra step.
- `O(n)` - Linear. Loop through array once. Double input = double time.
- `O(n log n)` - Merge sort, quick sort. The sweet spot for sorting.
- `O(n²)` - Quadratic. Nested loops. Double input = 4x slower.
- `O(2^n)` - Exponential. Brute force. Don't.

### Example:
```cpp
// O(1)
int x = arr[5];

// O(n)
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { ... }

// O(n²)
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) { ... }
```


